641:
Pursuant to the authority conferred by article IX, section II, paragraph IV of the 1982 constitution of the State of Georgia and to guide development in accordance with existing and future needs; to protect, promote, and improve public health, safety, morals, convenience, order, appearance, prosperity, and the general welfare; to ensure the adequate provision of light and air; to promote safety from fire and other dangers; to facilitate economical and adequate provision of public facilities such as transportation, water, sewerage, education, recreation, sanitation, and other public requirements; with reasonable consideration of the character of each area and its suitability for particular uses, and with a view toward promoting desirable living conditions and sustained stability of living areas, protecting property against blight, conserving the value of land and buildings, securing economy in governmental expenditures, and encouraging the most appropriate use of land, buildings, and structures; the city council does ordain and enact into law the following regulations:
(1) Dividing the city into districts and establishing the boundaries thereof;
(2) Regulating the locations, height, bulk, and sizes of buildings and other structures;
(3) Regulating the percentage of lots which may be covered;
(4) Regulating the sizes of yards, courts, and other open spaces;
(5) Regulating the density and distribution of the population;
(6) Regulating the uses and locations of buildings, structures, and land for trade, industry, residence, recreation, agriculture, conservation, public activities, airports, water supply, sanitation, and protection against floods;
(7) Providing for the method of administration and amendment of these regulations;
(8) Defining the powers and duties of the planning and zoning commission and board of zoning appeals with respect to these regulations;
(9) Defining certain terms used herein; and
(10) Providing penalties for violations of these regulations.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

661:
The following uses shall be permitted in the D-1 district:
(1) Agriculture, horticulture, animal husbandry (excluding feed lots, hog operations, dairy farming, and commercial chicken operations), forestry, and similar agriculturally-related uses;
(2) Single-family dwellings;
(3) Accessory buildings and uses of structures customarily incidental to any permitted use, including buildings used for agricultural purposes, provided that such structures shall not be less than five feet from any property line;
(4) Customary home occupations;
(5) Special exceptions, as prescribed in section 32-200;
(6) A lesser number of animals (family pets) are allowed to be raised in the restricted areas of the city.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

665:
(a) Purpose. The purpose of the R-1 district is to establish a low-density, single-family residential district and to protect property in the districts from the depreciating effects of incompatible land uses.
(b) Permitted uses. The following uses shall be permitted in the R-1 district:
(1) Single-family dwellings;
(2) Accessory buildings and uses of structures customarily incidental to any permitted use, provided such structures are located in the rear yard, not less than five feet from the rear property line, and not less than five feet from any other property line;
(3) Customary home occupations;
(4) Special exceptions, as prescribed in section 32-200.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

667:
(a) Purpose. The purpose of the R-3 district is to establish a medium- to high-density residential district and to protect property in the districts from the depreciating effects of incompatible land uses.
(b) Permitted uses. The following uses shall be permitted in the R-3 district:
(1) Any use permitted in R-1 residential districts;
(2) Accessory buildings, section 32-88(b)(2);
(3) Rest homes, convalescent homes, nursing homes, churches, and schools that are existing on February 2, 1993, are grandfathered into the present district; however, for future development, no building in connection with such facility shall be closer than ten feet from the lot line; section 32-200, special use exceptions;
(4) Residential group developments including single-family, two-family, and multifamily residence, such as apartment and condominium developments;
(5) Special exceptions, as prescribed in section 32-200.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

673:
The following uses shall be permitted in the P-1 district:
(1) Any use permitted in the R-2 district;
(2) Physicians, lawyers, engineers, architects, accountants, and similar professional uses which may occupy an entire building or group of buildings;
(3) Businesses which are incidental to the above professional practices, e.g., prescription shops and optical sales;
(4) Accessory buildings, section 32-88(b)(2);
(5) Signs, subject to the provisions of section 32-202;
(6) Special exceptions, as prescribed in section 32-200.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

677:
(a) Purpose. The C-1 district is intended to be a general commercial zone to provide a wide variety of commercial and service facilities.
(b) Permitted uses. The following uses shall be permitted in the C-1 district:
(1) Any use permitted in the P-1 district;
(2) Financial institutions;
(3) Retail establishments, including those dealing in used merchandise and used automobiles, but excluding junk yards and salvage yards;
(4) Personal service establishments;
(5) Eating and drinking establishments;
(6) Commercial establishments;
(7) Hotels and motels;
(8) Indoor theaters and amusement establishments, including bowling alleys, health studios, video game rooms, and similar indoor entertainment centers;
(9) Gasoline service stations, including automobile service and repair, but excluding junk yards;
(10) Veterinary clinics, provided that all animals shall be kept inside enclosed buildings;
(11) Accessory buildings and uses of structures customarily incidental to any permitted use, provided such structures are located in the rear yard and not less than five feet from any property line;
(12) Signs, subject to the provisions in section 32-202;
(13) Special exceptions, as prescribed in section 32-200.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

678:
(a) Purpose. The C-2 district is intended to be utilized by those commercial uses which serve community and regional shopping needs as well as the traveling public. These commercial uses occupy relatively large sites and require access to major thoroughfares. It is intended that this district be located at major road intersections.
(b) Permitted uses. The following uses shall be permitted in the C-2 district:
(1) Any use permitted in the C-1 district;
(2) Radio and television stations;
(3) Wholesaling, warehousing, bulk storage, supply, and distribution operations;
(4) Truck and bus terminals, freight and passenger terminals;
(5) Mobile home sales lots, new farm equipment sales lots, boat sales lots, and other lots for the sale of large items which cannot reasonably be stored indoors, but excluding the display of scrap, salvage, or junk products;
(6) Distributors of gasoline, fuel oil, agricultural chemicals and fertilizers, and liquefied petroleum gases;
(7) Accessory buildings and uses of structures customarily incidental to any permitted use, provided such structures are located in the rear yard and not less than five feet from any property line;
(8) Signs, subject to the provisions in section 32-202;
(9) Special exceptions, as prescribed in section 32-200.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

682:
The following uses shall be permitted in the I-1 district:
(1) Any use permitted in the C-2 district;
(2) Manufacturing plants, processing plants, and industrial uses which do not produce obnoxious noises, vibrations, smoke, gas, fumes, odors, dust or other similar conditions which would be incompatible with the character of the district or with development outside the district;
(3) Retail or wholesale business outlets operated as part of a manufacturing establishment;
(4) Public utilities, distribution lines, transformer stations, water tanks and towers, and telephone exchanges;
(5) Business offices associated with and secondary to individual industrial uses within the district;
(6) Automotive repair garages, which should limit the number of cars stored, with emphasis on upkeep and a clean-looking environment;
(7) Trade schools and technical schools engaging in activities of an industrial nature;
(8) Junkyards, salvage yards, scrap yards, and second-hand material operations, which are to provide screening and have enough land space to accommodate their needs;
(9) Laundry and dry cleaning processing plants;
(10) Veterinary clinics, kennels, and animal hospitals;
(11) Lumber and building supply yards, offices and yards for storage of contractor equipment and supplies, and outdoor storage of materials, supplies, or equipment;
(12) Scientific, technical, and medical laboratories and research facilities;
(13) Signs, subject to the provisions in section 32-202;
(14) Special exceptions as prescribed in section 32-200;
(15) Accessory buildings and uses of structures customarily incidental to any permitted use, provided such structures are located in the rear yard not less than 25 feet from the rear property line and not less than five feet from any other property line.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

685:
The following special use exceptions may be permitted in a zone where such uses are deemed essential or desirable to the public convenience or welfare and are in harmony with the various elements or objectives of this article:
(1) Churches and other places of worship;
(2) Public and private schools which offer general educational courses;
(3) Buildings used by federal, state, county, or city governments;
(4) Parks, public and semi-public grounds for games and sports;
(5) Clubs, lodges, and civic and fraternal organizations;
(6) Public utilities, distribution lines, transformer stations, water tanks and towers, and telephone exchanges, provided that when these uses are located in a residential area:
a. No vehicles or equipment are stored on the premises, unless they comply with all other regulations;
b. All district yard requirements are met;
c. The lot is suitably landscaped;
d. All such uses are designed so as not to cause interference with radio .....television reception;
e. Animals, pursuant to section 32-64(6);
(7) Rest homes, convalescent homes, orphanages, sanitariums, hospitals, and hospice and extended care;
(8) Funeral homes to be in commercial or business zones;
(9) Cemeteries, pursuant to section 32-204(d).
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

686:
No structure hereinafter shall be erected or any of the uses listed in this section shall be established unless at least the minimum number of automobile parking spaces as specified in this section are provided. Each parking space shall have an area of at least 200 square feet, plus necessary maneuvering and turning space. All off-street parking, except for single-family residential uses, shall be designed and arranged so as to prevent vehicles from backing onto a public street, road, or highway. Off-street parking spaces shall be provided as follows:
(1) Auto sales and repair: One space for each 150 square feet of auto repair or maintenance space;
(2) Churches, synagogues, or other places of worship: One space for each four seats in the main assembly room or sanctuary;
(3) Gasoline filling stations: Three spaces for each grease rack or similar facility, plus one space for each employee on the shift with the highest employment;
(4) Hospitals, nursing homes, or similar institutions: One space for each two beds intended for patients, not including bassinets, plus one space for each three employees on the shift with the highest employment;
(5) Industries: One space for each three employees on the shift with the highest employment;
(6) Funeral homes: One space for each four seats in the chapel, one additional space for each two employees, one additional space for each resident family, and one additional space for each funeral vehicle;
(7) Motels, hotels, or similar uses: One space for each room to be let, plus one space for each two employees on the shift with the highest employment;
(8) Offices, including banking institutions, but excluding doctors' and dentists' offices: One space for each 300 square feet of gross floor area;
(9) Offices for doctors and dentists: Seven spaces for each doctor or dentist;
(10) Places of public assembly, lodges, and clubs: One space for each four seats of maximum seating capacity in the main assembly room;
(11) Restaurants or similar eating establishments: One space for each four seats for patron use and one additional space for each two employees on the shift with the highest employment;
(12) Residences, single-family, duplex, and multifamily: Two spaces for each dwelling unit;
(13) Retail and services businesses: One space for each 300 square feet of sales floor area;
(14) Elementary, junior high and middle schools: One space for each classroom and administrative office;
(15) Senior high and vocational schools: One space for each four seats in the main auditorium or assembly room;
(16) Shopping centers: One space for each 150 square feet of gross floor area;
(17) Wholesaling and warehousing: One space for each three employees on the shift with the highest employees.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

687:
This section is intended to regulate the use of signs, billboards, and other outdoor advertising structures, and to comply with state advertising regulations.
(1) General provisions. In any zoning district, the following shall apply:
a. Signs may be erected within required front yard setbacks, and five feet off property line;
b. No portion of any sign shall overhang any public right-of-way;
c. No sign shall be erected or maintained in a manner which materially impedes visibility of or from automobiles or pedestrians, or which creates a traffic safety hazard;
d. Signs which imitate warning signals or emergency flashing lights or could otherwise be confused with an authorized traffic control or emergency sign, signal, or device are prohibited.
(2) Types of signs not subject to regulations. The following types of signs are not subject to sign regulations outlined in this section:
a. Signs not exceeding one square foot in area and bearing only property numbers, post office route and box numbers, or names of occupants on premises;
b. Flags and insignias of any government, except when used as part of commercial display or advertisement;
c. Legal notices, informational, or directional signs erected by governmental bodies, which are required to be erected by governmental bodies;
d. Integral decorative or architectural features of buildings or grounds except letters, trademarks, moving parts, moving or apparently moving lights, or advertising in any form;
e. Signs directing and guiding traffic on private property, but bearing no advertising matter.
(3) Temporary signs. Temporary signs are permitted in any zone, provided that they are non-illuminated and conform to the following provisions:
a. Temporary signs not exceeding 40 square feet in area announcing a land subdivision development are permitted on the premises of the subdivision. Such signs shall be set back not less than ten feet from any property line, and shall be spaced not less than 300 feet apart. Such signs shall be kept in a good state of repair, and shall be removed no later than 30 days after such time as 75 percent of the lots are conveyed.
b. One sign, not exceeding 60 square feet in area, and displaying the names of the building, the contractors, the architects, the engineers, the owners, and the financial agency, selling agency, and development agency is permitted upon the premises of any work under construction, alteration, or removal. The sign shall be removed no later than 30 days after completion of the project.
c. Signs, not exceeding 15 square feet in area, and displaying the names of house painters, craftspersons, contractors, and others involved in the repair, remodeling, or improvements are permitted on the premises on which the work is taking place. The signs shall be removed no later than seven days after the completion of the work.
d. In advertising any property for sale, rent, or lease, temporary signs may be erected. Such signs shall not exceed a surface area of five square feet per 100 feet of street frontage.
(4) Signs in residential zones. In residential zones, the following regulations shall apply:
a. For home occupations or professional offices located in residences, one non-illuminated sign, not to exceed two square feet of sign area, is permitted;
b. For group housing developments, apartments, or other multifamily residences, one identification sign, not to exceed 30 square feet, is permitted for each separate street frontage;
c. For other permitted nonresidential uses:
1. Not more than two identification signs with combined surface area not exceeding 20 square feet; and
2. Not more than two bulletin or notice boards with combined surface area not exceeding 30 square feet.
d. In single-family or duplex subdivisions, not more than two permanent identification signs are permitted per entrance totaling not more than 30 square feet.
(5) Signs in other zones. In all other zones, not more than two sign structures with combined sign surface area not to exceed one square foot per foot of street frontage is permitted for each separate street frontage.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), 2-2-1993)

694:
A planned unit development (PUD) is defined as a development project comprehensively planned as an entity with an overall site plan which permits flexibility in building siting, mixtures of land uses, and provision of open spaces. A PUD shall be primarily residential in character, and may include the following:
(1) Single-family detached dwellings;
(2) Single-family attached dwellings such as townhouses, condominiums, and duplexes;
(3) Multifamily dwellings;
(4) Public or semi-public institutions such as schools, churches, and community or club facilities;
(5) Recreational facilities, including swimming pools, golf courses, tennis courts, and other recreational facilities intended for the primary use of the residents of the development;
(6) Open spaces;
(7) Commercial, retail, and professional uses subject to the following limitations:
a. The commercial, retail, or professional use is designed to primarily serve the residents of the development;
b. The uses shall not have adverse effects on the residential uses within the development or in adjacent developments, or create traffic hazards to pedestrian or vehicular traffic.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), § 11-4.2, 2-2-1993)

698:
Each planned unit development (PUD) shall be subject to independent review and approval by the planning and zoning commission. This review process shall conform in general to the city subdivision regulations. In addition, the following requirements must be met:
(1) The developer shall submit a site plan to the planning and zoning commission for review and approval.
(2) The planning and zoning commission shall approve or disapprove the site plan within 30 days after submittal. The site plan shall include the following:
a. Total acreage in the tract proposed for the PUD;
b. Street access and street layout;
c. Arrangement of existing and proposed buildings and structures;
d. Existing and proposed land uses throughout the tract;
e. Topography by contours at intervals of at least five feet and extending at least 200 feet outside the tract;
f. Name, date, north arrow, and graphic scale;
g. A vicinity map showing the relationship of the proposed PUD to surrounding development;
h. The locations and dimensions of off-street parking spaces;
i. The locations and sizes of all proposed utility and storm drainage facilities, and solid waste collection points;
j. Such other information as the planning and zoning commission may deem necessary because of physical characteristics peculiar to the particular development.
The developer pays for water and sewerage hook-ups, for paving and to keep updated to accommodate his facility.
(Ord. No. 1993-02(1), § 11-4.6, 2-2-1993)
